  \"ChemCPA\",\n          \"SAMS-VAE\",\n          \"Contrastive learning-based perturbation predictors\",\n          \"Graph neural network-based gene regulatory models\",\n          \"Linear or ridge regression on pseudobulk differential expression\",\n          \"KNN or mean control baseline\"\n        ],\n        \"strengths\": [\n          \"scGen and CPA provide out-of-distribution perturbation predictions using latent space arithmetic\",\n          \"GEARS incorporates gene-gene relationships via co-expression and Gene Ontology graphs\",\n          \"CPA and ChemCPA are scalable and support multiple perturbations and cell lines\",\n          \"Variational autoencoders latent space can capture biological variation beyond technical noise\",\n          \"Graph-based models can prioritize genes with known regulatory interactions\",\n          \"Pseudobulk mean baselines are computationally cheap and can perform well for top DE gene ranking\"\n        ],\n        \"limitations\": [\n          \"Many VAE-based methods struggle with unseen perturbations when effect directions are not represented in training\",\n          \"GEARS relies on prior gene-gene interaction graphs that may be incomplete or biased in K562\",\n          \"scGen assumes additive perturbation effects in latent space, which may fail for strong CRISPRi knockdowns\",\n          \"Most deep models are evaluated on seen perturbations and overestimate generalization\",\n          \"Biological interpretability is limited because latent perturbations are not directly mapped to regulatory mechanisms\",\n          \"Computational cost of hyperparameter tuning and retraining per perturbation can be prohibitive\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_relevance\": \"The most biologically relevant methods explicitly model gene regulatory networks, use cell type context, or preserve the covariance structure of the transcriptome under perturbation, because CRISPRi perturbations in K562 primarily alter downstream gene programs rather than producing arbitrary expression shifts.\"\n      },\n      \"model_comparison\": {\n        \"comparison_criteria\": [\n          \"Prediction accuracy on unseen perturbations\",\n          \"Pearson correlation between predicted and observed perturbation delta\",\n          \"Top-20 differentially expressed gene recovery\",\n          \"MSE on log-normalized expression means\",\n          \"Biological interpretability of learned perturbation effects\",\n          \"Training time and GPU memory\",\n          \"Scalability to full genome expression profiles\",\n          \"Robustness to technical and biological variation\"\n        ],\n        \"performance_metrics\": [\n          \"pearson_delta\",\n          \"MSE\",\n          \"Top-20 DE gene MSE\",\n          \"Top-20 DE gene PCC\",\n          \"Top-20 DE gene R2\",\n          \"Rank correlation of perturbation effect sizes\",\n          \"Calibration of predicted expression uncertainty\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_interpretability\": \"Models that decompose perturbation effects into gene network modules or rely on known TF-target interactions are more interpretable than black-box VAEs. For CRISPRi K562 data, a model that can associate a perturbation with its target gene and downstream expression signature is preferable.\",\n        \"computational_requirements\": \"Pseudobulk linear models require minimal compute, while CPA, scGEN, and GEARS require GPU training but are feasible on a single modern GPU for the Norman K562 dataset. Full gene-level Graph Neural Networks are expensive and require careful sparsification.\"\n      },\n      \"recommendations\": {\n        \"model_selection\": [\n          \"Use a strong pseudobulk mean/control baseline as a lower-bound reference\",\n          \"Train CPA as a robust perturbation autoencoder for unseen perturbations\",\n          \"Consider a gene-regulatory graph-informed model such as GEARS for top DE gene prioritization\",\n          \"Ensemble a VAE-based predictor with a regularized linear model to improve robustness\"\n        ],\n        \"implementation_priority\": [\n          \"First build a reliable control mean reference from all unperturbed K562 cells\",\n          \"Then implement a simple nearest-neighbor or ridge baseline on pseudobulk means\",\n          \"Then train CPA or scGen with hyperparameter tuning on held-out perturbations\",\n          \"Add graph-based gene relationship regularization if top DE gene recovery is insufficient\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_validation\": \"Validate predictions by checking whether known target genes of each CRISPRi perturbation show the expected down-regulation, and whether top predicted DE genes are enriched in pathways associated with the targeted gene.\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"evaluation_framework\": {\n      \"metrics\": {\n        \"primary_metrics\": [\n          \"pearson_delta correlation per perturbation\",\n          \"MSE between predicted and true log-normalized mean expression\",\n          \"MSE on top-20 DE genes\",\n          \"PCC on top-20 DE genes\",\n          \"R2 on top-20 DE genes\"\n        ],\n        \"secondary_metrics\": [\n          \"Spearman correlation of perturbation effect sizes\",\n          \"Precision and recall of top-20 DE genes\",\n          \"Root mean squared error of log fold change\",\n          \"Cosine similarity of perturbation delta vectors\",\n          \"Area under the precision-recall curve for DE gene detection\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_metrics\": [\n          \"Enrichment of known target gene knockdown among low predicted expression\",\n          \"Pathway enrichment of top predicted DE genes\",\n          \"Correlation of predicted expression with TF regulon activity\",\n          \"Consistency with known K562 CRISPRi effect directions\",\n          \"Cell cycle and stress signature confounding score\"\n        ],\n        \"validation_metrics\": [\n          \"Cross-perturbation holdout MSE\",\n          \"Within-replicate control variance baseline\",\n          \"Permutation test p-value for delta correlation\",\n          \"Calibration error of prediction intervals\",\n          \"Generalization gap between seen and unseen perturbations\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"validation_strategy\": {\n        \"cross_validation\": \"Use stratified leave-out perturbation cross-validation, where each fold holds out a random set of CRISPRi perturbations, ensuring that all cells from a perturbation are excluded from training. Repeat with multiple seeds to estimate variance.\",\n        \"biological_validation\": \"Compare predicted knockdown effects against independent CRISPRi target gene signatures from the K562 literature, and against known gene dependency scores such as DepMap CRISPR effect for the targeted genes.\",\n        \"independent_testing\": \"Hold out perturbations from the provided dataset and score using Gene-Mila's evaluator. Additionally, if available, test on other K562 CRISPRi screens with overlapping perturbations but different batches.\",\n        \"robustness_testing\": \"Evaluate sensitivity to downsampled cell counts, different normalization schemes, different control samples, and inclusion of batch or cell cycle covariates. Measure performance degradation under noise injection in expression data.\"\n      },\n      \"benchmarks\": {\n        \"baseline_models\": [\n          \"Control mean expression predictor\",\n          \"Mean expression across all training perturbations\",\n          \"Ridge regression on one-hot perturbation labels\",\n          \"PCA + nearest neighbor pseudobulk predictor\",\n          \"Simple MLP on target gene identity\"\n        ],\n        \"state_of_the_art\": [\n          \"CPA\",\n          \"scGen\",\n          \"scVIDR\",\n          \"GEARS\",\n          \"ChemCPA\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_benchmarks\": [\n          \"Known K562 CRISPRi target gene knockdown signatures\",\n          \"DepMap gene effect scores\",\n          \"Enrichment of target gene regulons\",\n          \"Transcription factor perturbation response from literature\"\n        ],\n        \"performance_targets\": [\n          \"Mean pearson_delta above 0.5 for held-out perturbations\",\n          \"Top-20 DE gene recovery precision above 0.3\",\n          \"MSE lower than control variance baseline\",\n          \"Top-20 DE gene PCC above 0.4\",\n          \"No systematic sign error in predicted target gene expression\"\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    \"performance_analysis\": {\n      \"model_performance\": {\n        \"accuracy_metrics\": [\n          \"Pearson delta per perturbation\",\n          \"Global MSE across all genes\",\n          \"Top-20 DE gene MSE/PCC/R2\",\n          \"Rank accuracy of perturbation effect sizes\",\n          \"Calibration of predicted mean expression\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_relevance\": \"Model predictions should recover the known CRISPRi target gene as down-regulated and identify downstream transcriptional programs consistent with K562 biology. High global MSE can be acceptable if the top DE gene structure is correct.\",\n        \"generalization\": \"Unseen perturbation generalization is the central challenge. Models relying only on perturbation embedding without gene network constraints tend to memorize observed perturbations and perform poorly on held-out targets. Regularization and biological priors improve generalization.\",\n        \"robustness\": \"Robustness is assessed by performance stability across random seeds, cell subsamples, and held-out perturbation subsets. The best models maintain high top DE gene recovery even when mean expression is noisy.\"\n      },\n      \"comparative_analysis\": {\n        \"baseline_comparison\": \"A simple control mean baseline has low MSE but zero pearson_delta, while ridge regression captures some shared perturbation effects but generalizes poorly. Deep latent variable models outperform baselines for top DE gene recovery if properly regularized.\",\n        \"state_of_the_art_comparison\": \"CPA and scGEN generally achieve higher pearson_delta than linear baselines on unseen perturbations. GEARS can improve top DE gene ranking through gene-gene priors, but its benefit is smaller when the perturbation target is known and the effect is strong.\",\n        \"biological_comparison\": \"Graph-regularized models produce more biologically coherent top DE genes and less off-target noise, while pure expression autoencoders may predict smooth but less specific changes. Biological validation shows that target gene knockdown is recovered more often by graph-informed models.\",\n        \"computational_comparison\": \"Linear baselines train in seconds, CPA and scGEN train in minutes to hours on GPU, GEARS with large gene graphs can take several hours and high memory. For a single K562 dataset, CPA offers the best accuracy-per-compute tradeoff.\"\n      },\n      \"strengths_weaknesses\": {\n        \"strengths\": [\n          \"Deep latent variable models can learn reusable perturbation effect directions\",\n          \"Graph-based regularization improves biological coherence\",\n          \"Pseudobulk averaging reduces technical noise and stabilizes predictions\",\n          \"Conditioning on perturbation identity is a strong prior in CRISPRi screens\"\n        ],\n        \"weaknesses\": [\n          \"Most models have limited extrapolation to unseen gene targets\",\n          \"Global MSE is dominated by highly expressed genes and may miss subtle DE genes\",\n          \"Uncertainty quantification is often absent or poorly calibrated\",\n          \"Hyperparameters and data normalization strongly influence performance\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_insights\": [\n          \"CRISPRi effect is strongly target-gene-dependent and dose-dependent\",\n          \"Perturbation responses in K562 are largely captured by a low-dimensional latent space\",\n          \"Gene co-expression and TF regulon structure provide useful inductive bias for unseen perturbations\"\n        ],\n        \"technical_limitations\": [\n          \"Cell count per perturbation is often low, limiting accurate mean estimation\",\n          \"Batch effects and cell cycle state can confound perturbation effects\",\n          \"Log normalization biases rankings of lowly expressed genes\",\n          \"Training and evaluation on the same gene set limits generalization to new gene panels\"\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    \"improvement_suggestions\": {\n      \"model_enhancements\": {\n        \"architectural_improvements\": [\n          \"Use a hierarchical variational autoencoder with separate latent variables for perturbation and cell state\",\n          \"Integrate a gene regulatory network or co-expression graph into the decoder\",\n          \"Introduce a contrastive objective to align perturbation latent representations with known TF regulons\",\n          \"Add a differentiable top-DE loss that emphasizes high-effect genes during training\"\n        ],\n        \"training_optimizations\": [\n          \"Use adversarial or maximum mean discrepancy regularization to remove batch and cell cycle effects\",\n          \"Train with a masked gene reconstruction objective to improve unseen gene effect transfer\",\n          \"Apply early stopping on held-out perturbations to avoid overfitting to seen perturbations\",\n          \"Calibrate uncertainty using dropout or deep ensembles\"\n        ],\n        \"biological_interpretability\": [\n          \"Penalize perturbations that conflict with known TF-target signatures\",\n          \"Project latent perturbation effects onto gene network modules for pathway-level interpretation\",\n          \"Report per-gene effect sizes and confidence intervals for all held-out perturbations\",\n          \"Use SHAP or integrated gradients to explain top predicted DE genes\"\n        ],\n        \"compu